00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.119 align:start position:0%
 
the global lockdown and restrictions  have created a situation in which women  at risk of abuse may feel like they no  longer have a way out here's a look at  some of these statistics from around the  world now the French government had  offered to pay for hotel rooms for  domestic violence victims after reports  of abuse jumped by 36 percent since the  lockdown began two women have been  murdered India saw abuse cases double in  the first week of restrictions on  movement nationwide that's according to  the National Commission for women in  Australia internet searches for domestic  violence support networks rose by 75  percent the National domestic abuse  helpline in the UK has experienced a 25%  increase in calls and online requests  since the lockdown came in and to  newzeas women's affairs minister says  abuse cases have gone up at five times  the usual rate since a curfew was  imposed in March  well the giordano is director of  training at Men's stopping violence he  joins us on skype from Atlanta Georgia  thank you for your time so we are  talking now about violence primarily  against women while under lockdown how  do you do your job engage with men to  end violence against women during this  time well first thank you for having me  on this morning so our job has become a  little bit more complicated now that  we're all working virtually many of our  programs our intervention programs with  men who have used abusive behavior are  now transitioning and being conducted  virtually in online classes as well as  our general engagement effort so our  mission have been stopping violence is  to engage communities and men to take  action to end violence against women and  so basically we're now a virtual  engagement organization but I guess it's  going to be different difficult or  perhaps impossible isn't it to reach  those households that are under lockdown  that haven't engaged with anyone before  it's going to be difficult to reach  those people this is quite an unusual  time with a global pandemic absolutely  there's there's a combination of  amic's right now that are increasing  rates of domestic violence so we know  that stress levels have gone up people  are financially strained people are  losing their jobs people's normal lives  are being disrupted but we also know  that when isolation when when victims  are isolated they have less recourse and  so instances of violence go up we also  know that when there isn't a consequence  for an abusers use of violence then they  can abuse with impunity in there and I  think that is what is happening now the  abuse person isn't able to get out a  call for help because the abuser is  always there with them how do we include  the male perspective I mean when I saw  we were speaking to someone from men  stopping violence it sort of raised my  Albert eyebrows because I'm interested  in and how do you think we include the  male perspective and the male role when  talking about violence against women  without shifting the focus away from  women's voices from women's stories from  women's experiences who are primarily  the victims well we have to acknowledge  that if women are primarily the victim  then is likely that men are primarily  the perpetrators but most men are not  perpetrators of violence but most men  also are not taking responsibility for  helping to end or reduce the occurrences  of violence so our message to men is  even if you're not one who's engaged in  abusive or controlling behavior you have  a responsibility in your community to  take action to speak up to confront men  in your community who may be engaging in  those behaviors one of the things that  we're offering currently is resources  and help over the phone so that anyone  who is using abuse can call us and we  can help with with tools or options  anyone who knows someone who is using  abuse can call us when we can  then think about how they may intervene  or have a conversation with that men so  it's it's it is a issue that primarily  affects women but men have a huge role  that they could be playing if if they  were willing to step up and that's  really the goal of our organization is  helping men to do that thank you for  your time there leads Yordano director  of training at men stopping violence  thank you  Kavita Krishnan is an activist and  secretary of the all-india progressive  women's association she joins us on  skype from New Delhi thank you for your  time just asked what are you seeing in  India hundreds of women are reaching out  put that in perspective for us the  lockdown was announced without any  warning so India had warning of the  approaching kovat 19 crisis since  January 30th but the government did not  tell people that any lockdown might be  in the offing and they suddenly  announced this 21-day lockdown that has  left a lot of women's who suffer  domestic violence taken by surprise so  they have not been able to secure  themselves or to leave the homes so they  find themselves trapped with their  abuser in many cases and I think that  the number of cases must be much higher  than what the National Commission of  women is talking about because India  does not have any national hotline or  any state-level hotline for domestic  violence survivors so this is a very bad  situation on the and there's also other  things compounding the situation which  is in India in general even when there  is no lockdown the tendency for the  police and other you know government  authorities to take domestic violence  very lightly or to make excuses for it  or to ask the woman complainant to go  back to the house of the abuser and not  expect any help is extremely common and  now clearly it is more common I mean I  only can speak from anecdotal experience  because of my own organizations  experience in dealing with domestic  violence calls during the lockdown but I  can tell you that the pressures you know  it's like it's explosive it's a it's an  explosive situation because there are  tremendous tensions because of the  locked  so loss of work loss of survival  resources greater domestic burdens on  women who are dealing with this  household situation where there is less  food and it's more difficult to survive  and in that situation may you know  abusive men have tended to take it out  on women inside the household especially  husbands against wives and so this is an  extremely concerning situation yes what  are the options then for women in India  who need health very few options exist  as of now because in general India's see  it's it's like what Miss Curcio Cortez  says in the u.s. that the pandemic has  poured gasoline on our existing troubles  so India in general has very poor  domestic violence you know help so we do  have a law against domestic violence and  there are supposed to be respondents to  help women who are in trouble but we  have very we have no shelters so the  state-run shelters tend to be viewed as  jails by the women so their women are  not treated well there and so we  basically have very little public  spending on shelters in general or in  other other forms of help and in this  lockdown situation naturally the kind of  help and resources have gone down  because here even if you need an amp and  I say this from experience even if you  need an ambulance in a hurry for someone  who is suddenly ill you can't get it in  the city of Delhi which is India's  capital city all right now just imagine  how you know what women have gone  through when they have had trouble if  they call the police the police say what  is this we are in the middle of this  pandemic and you're complaining about a  quarrel with your husband don't bother  so recently a note recently some  feminists put out a little video  explainer in India about domestic uses  prior to the lockdown about how the  police tend to play down domestic  violence and make excuses for it and  this is again something which is all the  more excesses created during the  lockdown a lot of work to be done thank  you for your time Kavita Krishnan from  the All India progressive Women's  Association

